The flow of the entire system
Here's the journey of a single player action from click to confirmed state change:
Step 1 — Client connects: Player opens the app. WebSocket handshake establishes a persistent connection. Server assigns a session token and player ID. Connection is registered in the connection pool.
Step 2 — Matchmaking: Player requests a match. Matchmaking service checks for an open room with the right parameters (player count, game mode, skill bracket). If found, assigns the player. If not, creates a new room and puts the player in the waiting queue. When the room fills, a GAME_START event broadcasts to all players.
Step 3 — Player sends an action: Player clicks "move unit to (3,5)." Client immediately applies this optimistically to local state (so the UI feels instant). Simultaneously, client sends a message: { type: "ACTION", payload: { unitId: "u1", to: [3,5] }, seq: 12, clientTimestamp: 1716300000123 }.
Step 4 — Server receives and queues: The incoming message handler pushes this into the room's action queue. It does NOT process it yet — that's the game loop's job.
Step 5 — Game tick fires: Every 50ms, the game loop processes the queue. Actions are sorted by their logical timestamp. For each action, the anti-cheat validator runs: is this unit owned by this player? Is (3,5) a legal destination given the unit's movement range? Is the player currently in a stunned state? If validation fails, the action is rejected and the client gets a correction message.
Step 6 — State reconciliation: If valid, the action mutates the authoritative game state. The new state is diffed against the previous tick's state to produce a delta (only changed fields — sending the full state every tick would be wasteful).
Step 7 — Broadcast: The delta + new sequence number broadcasts to all connected players in the room. Players whose optimistic state matches the server state feel nothing. Players whose optimistic state diverged (because they acted while another action was in-flight) receive a correction and roll back to server truth.
Step 8 — Reconnection: A player drops. Their session is marked DISCONNECTED but not removed for 30 seconds (grace period). When they reconnect with the same session token, the server sends them the current full state snapshot + all missed sequence numbers since their last acknowledged seq. Client fast-forwards through the event log
